 and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Missouri remains a Republican-leaning state in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of about nine points toward the GOP and a string of comfortable Republican results in recent cycles. The 2028 race is open because the incumbent president is term-limited, and neither party has settled on a nominee; coverage so far involves primary speculation, including Josh Hawley declining a run and Eric Schmitt drawing vice-presidential buzz, rather than the general election itself.\n\nNo general-election polls or expert ratings are available, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamentals. That leaves wide uncertainty: a national environment shifting a few points either way, or a nominee-driven dynamic, could move Missouri's margin meaningfully. As things stand, the Republican candidate is clearly favored but not safely so, with the state expected to lean roughly nine points Republican.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1545, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2074, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1871, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1883, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2013, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.183, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2061, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.204, "margin": -9.0}]}